WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:02.120
<v Maya>Nvidia keeps open‑source plans alive.

2
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.350
<v Maya>That, and sixteen more stories, on First Tick.

3
00:00:14.143 --> 00:00:15.740
<v Alex>This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.210
<v Alex>What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:18.730
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

6
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:19.900
<v Maya>And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.840
<v Maya>It's Wednesday, November 18th, and this is First Tick: the day's tech,

8
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.470
<v Maya>AI and market news in about half an hour, with why each story matters.

9
00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.900
<v Maya>Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.280
<v Alex>On today's show.

11
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.880
<v Alex>Nvidia keeps open‑source plans alive.

12
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.940
<v Alex>Nvidia rejects founder’s return offer.

13
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.620
<v Alex>Amazon grabs a slice of Anthropic’s future.

14
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.880
<v Alex>Waymo hits roadblock on freeways.

15
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.120
<v Alex>And Hydropower tanks are drying up.

16
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.460
<v Alex>Plus twelve more.

17
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:50.190
<v Alex>Let's get into it.

18
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.380
<v Maya>Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:53.380 --> 00:01:01.550
<v Maya>At Tuesday's close, the S&amp;P 500 finished at 7,341, down 1.0 percent.

20
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:07.650
<v Maya>The Nasdaq Composite was down 0.7 percent and the Dow was down 1.6 percent.

21
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.620
<v Alex>And in big tech, Tesla had the best day, up 0.1 percent,

22
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.340
<v Alex>while Microsoft was at the other end, down 2.3 percent.

23
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.000
<v Alex>That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.390
<v Alex>Jensen Huang is out, Collette Kress steps in.

25
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.720
<v Maya>She says the open‑source strategy stays, no change.

26
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.920
<v Alex>Hugging Face deal still on track.

27
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.620
<v Maya>$12.9 b, second‑largest purchase.

28
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.740
<v Alex>35‑fold token surge on OpenRouter.

29
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:40.130
<v Maya>3.5‑fold rise in last three months, still no comment.

30
00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.540
<v Alex>Nemotron now the most used U.S.

31
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.740
<v Alex>open model.

32
00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.520
<v Maya>It’s being built for long‑running agentic AI, no shift.

33
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.400
<v Maya>How does Nvidia plan to weave Hugging Face into its own stack?

34
00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:57.280
<v Alex>It’s all about scaling their GPU‑heavy inference pipelines so the models can run

35
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.660
<v Alex>end‑to‑end on Nvidia hardware, keeping the open‑weight aspect intact.

36
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.130
<v Maya>That sounds neat, but who’s actually going to fund it?

37
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.320
<v Alex>$12.9 b, that’s the price tag on Hugging Face.

38
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.600
<v Maya>And the token volume jump—35‑fold—where’s that coming from?

39
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.780
<v Alex>It’s from OpenRouter’s weekly stats; the surge shows developers are pulling more

40
00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.540
<v Alex>requests through open models.

41
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.740
<v Maya>So the acquisition is basically a bet on developers scaling out?

42
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.840
<v Alex>Exactly, the idea is to let the developer community build on top of Nvidia’s

43
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.220
<v Alex>hardware while keeping the models open.

44
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:40.610
<v Alex>I think the 35‑fold token jump just shows developers are hungry for any open model,

45
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.240
<v Alex>not a sign of a sustainable ecosystem.

46
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:44.580
<v Maya>Huh, that’s a sharp take.

47
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.120
<v Alex>So if the devs are just chasing traffic,

48
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:54.750
<v Alex>Nvidia’s $12.9 b buy might not pay off unless they actually get paid for inference.

49
00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.000
<v Maya>Right, the cost of scaling those GPU‑heavy pipelines is huge,

50
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.840
<v Maya>and the model owners still own the weights.

51
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.610
<v Alex>And yet, Collette’s line about “making intelligence more open” feels like rhetoric

52
00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.410
<v Alex>unless they lock in revenue streams.

53
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.660
<v Maya>It’s a bet on the market’s appetite for open tools; if the dev community keeps

54
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.420
<v Maya>buying GPU time, the money comes back.

55
00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:21.120
<v Alex>But with Nemotron already the top U.S.

56
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.850
<v Alex>open model, maybe they’re just riding a wave that could dry up.

57
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:31.180
<v Maya>That’s the risk, but the fact that Hugging Face serves 200,000 companies suggests a

58
00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.960
<v Maya>base that can sustain the spend.

59
00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.270
<v Maya>Nvidia's board just flat‑out rejected Huang's comeback offer,

60
00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.320
<v Maya>according to TechCrunch.

61
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:44.730
<v Alex>They said his resignation was voluntary after a personal conduct issue.

62
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.120
<v Maya>He posted on X asking if the board would let him return.

63
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:48.700
<v Alex>Really?

64
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:49.160
<v Alex>That’s bold.

65
00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.560
<v Maya>Board said they won't reinstate him, citing the conduct.

66
00:03:53.034 --> 00:03:56.180
<v Alex>Colette Kress, the CFO, is now interim CEO.

67
00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.780
<v Maya>They’re also hunting for a new permanent boss.

68
00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:00.400
<v Alex>And an SEC probe is on.

69
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.420
<v Maya>So the AI future stays in uncertain hands.

70
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.110
<v Alex>So, what’s the board’s exact wording on the “voluntary” part?

71
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:12.060
<v Maya>They said Huang stepped down after a personal conduct issue was disclosed,

72
00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.660
<v Maya>and the board accepted that resignation unanimously.

73
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.140
<v Alex>Independent investigation, right?

74
00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.780
<v Maya>Yeah, the board launched an independent probe into his activities,

75
00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:24.040
<v Maya>and the SEC is looking into Nvidia too.

76
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.840
<v Alex>Colette Kress stepping in—she’s CFO, right?

77
00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.800
<v Alex>And the board’s open to internal and external names?

78
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.920
<v Maya>That’s what the chairman said on the earnings call—no one’s ruling out either.

79
00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:38.460
<v Maya>So the board’s saying it was a voluntary resignation because of conduct.

80
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:39.600
<v Alex>Says who?

81
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.040
<v Alex>That’s the whole “voluntary” thing.

82
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.340
<v Maya>I think the board is protecting the brand, not the guy.

83
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:49.340
<v Alex>And Colette stepping in—she’s the CFO, so she’s got the numbers, not the hype.

84
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.240
<v Maya>The SEC probe is still on, so they’re not cooling the fire yet.

85
00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.390
<v Alex>It sounds like they’re keeping the tech moving,

86
00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.600
<v Alex>but the leadership shuffle could slow things.

87
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.900
<v Maya>At least they’re open to external hires, that could bring fresh ideas.

88
00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:07.660
<v Alex>Right, but the real question is whether the new CEO can keep the chips flowing.

89
00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.020
<v Alex>Maya, did Amazon just snag a chunk of Anthropic?

90
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:16.820
<v Maya>TechCrunch says Amazon owns 14.7% after IPO.

91
00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.240
<v Alex>That’s less than 15%, not a game‑changer?

92
00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:23.420
<v Maya>And Google’s at 13.7%, pretty close.

93
00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.620
<v Alex>Wait, they’re buying non‑voting shares?

94
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.800
<v Maya>Yes, from converted notes and a private placement.

95
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.240
<v Alex>Non‑voting—so no say in decisions.

96
00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.140
<v Maya>But they can convert to voting when they sell.

97
00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.660
<v Alex>Sounds like a big cloud‑service bet, huh?

98
00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:41.340
<v Maya>Any idea why Amazon kept those notes instead of converting everything now?

99
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.900
<v Alex>They’d hit 23.8% if they did, so maybe they’re waiting for a price rise before

100
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.490
<v Alex>swapping into voting stock.

101
00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.160
<v Maya>So the 14.7% comes from converting just part of the notes?

102
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.570
<v Alex>Yeah, they took a slice and got non‑voting shares,

103
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.560
<v Alex>which pay dividends and can turn into Class A when sold.

104
00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.540
<v Maya>Google’s 13.7% is from the same deal?

105
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.940
<v Alex>Exactly—plus a $10 billion private placement, matching Nvidia’s buy.

106
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.820
<v Maya>That’s a $26 billion block for the whole consortium.

107
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:21.600
<v Alex>And AWS will pocket revenue from Anthropic’s planned $100 billion cloud spend over

108
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.240
<v Alex>ten years.

109
00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.020
<v Alex>This feels like a bet on the cloud, not the AI itself.

110
00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.520
<v Maya>But the numbers are the same—around 15% each.

111
00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.440
<v Alex>Non‑voting though, so they’re not steering the ship.

112
00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.120
<v Maya>True, but they can switch to voting when they sell.

113
00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.840
<v Alex>I think they're keeping options open while riding the growth.

114
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:45.240
<v Maya>And with that $100 billion cloud spend, AWS is lining up a steady stream.

115
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.460
<v Alex>So it’s less about control, more about revenue.

116
00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.820
<v Maya>Exactly, the focus is on service contracts, not governance.

117
00:06:54.680 --> 00:07:00.260
<v Maya>Waymo’s California freeway rides are suspended after the DMV said they’re a safety

118
00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:00.680
<v Maya>hazard.

119
00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:01.780
<v Alex>What’s the real issue?

120
00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:06.260
<v Maya>Three cars hit construction zones on 101 and 280.

121
00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.460
<v Alex>Incidents, not crashes.

122
00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.560
<v Maya>Waymo now blocks freeways everywhere and will stay until it shows corrective action.

123
00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.720
<v Alex>Says the DMV, not the driver.

124
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.240
<v Maya>They’re still running surface‑street trips, over half a million per week.

125
00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.600
<v Alex>So it’s a test run, not a full stop.

126
00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:27.320
<v Alex>What was the DMV’s exact wording on the “effective corrective actions” they’re

127
00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:27.960
<v Alex>waiting for?

128
00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:33.140
<v Maya>They said Waymo must prove it can stop the “recurring incursions” into construction

129
00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:38.140
<v Maya>zones, after a prior restriction and fixes from a May 18 incident.

130
00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.640
<v Alex>So they’re basically saying the software still trips over cones?

131
00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:43.520
<v Maya>Not exactly.

132
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.860
<v Maya>It’s the system’s decision‑making that’s flagged, not a single driver.

133
00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:47.500
<v Alex>Says who?

134
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.840
<v Maya>The DMV’s own statement; it didn’t mention any external audit.

135
00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.140
<v Alex>And Waymo’s own reply?

136
00:07:53.561 --> 00:07:58.140
<v Maya>They pointed out no injuries, and that they’ll keep surface‑street ops running,

137
00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.610
<v Maya>serving over half a million trips weekly,

138
00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.040
<v Maya>while they work with regulators to get back on freeways.

139
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.900
<v Maya>So Waymo’s own data shows half a million trips a week—does that stop the DMV from

140
00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.620
<v Maya>pulling the plug?

141
00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.680
<v Alex>I think the DMV is worried about the system’s decision‑making, not the volume.

142
00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.860
<v Maya>The DMV is the only one calling it a hazard.

143
00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.160
<v Alex>They’re waiting for “effective corrective actions,” but no audit was mentioned,

144
00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:24.320
<v Alex>so who’s really checking?

145
00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:28.840
<v Maya>If it’s just software tweaks, that’s a lot of money for a few construction cones.

146
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:34.470
<v Alex>I’m not buying the idea that a few incidents can justify a nationwide freeze.

147
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.340
<v Maya>The freeze covers all markets,

148
00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.760
<v Maya>even where Waymo isn’t on freeways—does that hurt the business model?

149
00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.940
<v Alex>It’s a reminder that building a safe system is a longer process than a quick market

150
00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:46.440
<v Alex>roll‑out.

151
00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.340
<v Alex>Hydropower hit a record low this summer.

152
00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.240
<v Maya>28% drop from the 10‑year average.

153
00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.320
<v Alex>Why the dip, Maya?

154
00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:55.540
<v Maya>Drought and low reservoirs.

155
00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.470
<v Alex>Seriously?

156
00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.640
<v Maya>Lake Powell barely meets turbine level.

157
00:08:58.679 --> 00:09:00.160
<v Alex>That’s a 1% margin.

158
00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.740
<v Maya>Lake Mead is 9% above its limit.

159
00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.840
<v Alex>So the plants can barely run.

160
00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:07.020
<v Maya>Who’s talking about the snowpack percentage?

161
00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.200
<v Maya>That’s a lot of water dropping straight into the river.

162
00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:14.040
<v Alex>Yeah, but the river’s been running at record‑low levels lately.

163
00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.380
<v Maya>So the reservoirs are basically a drought?

164
00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.320
<v Alex>2026 is projected to be the lowest in over ten years.

165
00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.580
<v Maya>And what about the El Niño?

166
00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.440
<v Alex>It gave a 20‑foot boost to Lake Powell, but still not enough for full recovery.

167
00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:32.510
<v Alex>It feels like a warning sign for any grid that still relies on those dams.

168
00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.180
<v Maya>But the numbers show a 28% drop.

169
00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.300
<v Alex>A 1% margin at Lake Powell?

170
00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.420
<v Alex>That's barely enough to keep turbines spinning.

171
00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:45.460
<v Maya>And Lake Mead's 9% still above its limit, but that's not much leeway either.

172
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.440
<v Alex>So the plants are barely running.

173
00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.300
<v Maya>My read is that the whole system is on edge, not a green powerhouse.

174
00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:57.200
<v Alex>If the reservoirs stay low, we might see more reliance on batteries or other

175
00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:57.720
<v Alex>renewables.

176
00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:03.200
<v Maya>That shift could lift emissions, since the shortfall already added to global rises.

177
00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.977
<v Maya>Isomorphic Labs just raised five billion dollars.

178
00:10:08.425 --> 00:10:10.530
<v Alex>Greenoaks, Sequoia, and Andreessen led it.

179
00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.920
<v Maya>They’re using AlphaFold‑derived tech to design drugs.

180
00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.100
<v Alex>Hope it’s not just hype.

181
00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:20.460
<v Maya>They claim positive preclinical results in oncology and immunology.

182
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.190
<v Alex>Eli Lilly, Novartis, Johnson &amp; Johnson are on board.

183
00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.560
<v Maya>Demis Hassabis left DeepMind to focus on this.

184
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.540
<v Alex>He’s now chairman at DeepMind but still runs Isomorphic.

185
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.680
<v Maya>Bloomberg said they’re eyeing a fifty‑billion valuation.

186
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.960
<v Alex>What’s the engine actually doing with those simulations?

187
00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.750
<v Maya>It runs thousands of molecular dynamics runs,

188
00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:46.380
<v Maya>each checking binding pockets against target proteins fed from AlphaFold

189
00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:46.740
<v Maya>predictions.

190
00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:47.680
<v Alex>That fast?

191
00:10:48.222 --> 00:10:52.330
<v Maya>The preclinical hits came from oncology and immunology datasets,

192
00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:55.420
<v Maya>but no numbers yet; they just say positive results.

193
00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.820
<v Alex>Hold on, who’s funding the clinical push?

194
00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.620
<v Maya>Greenoaks and Sequoia are leading,

195
00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.360
<v Maya>but Andreessen Horowitz and the existing backers are all in,

196
00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:05.840
<v Maya>so the cash flow is secured.

197
00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.280
<v Alex>So it’s a mix of big pharma and venture?

198
00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:14.020
<v Maya>Exactly—Eli Lilly, Novartis, and Johnson &amp; Johnson have partnership letters,

199
00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:18.220
<v Maya>and the round keeps the same investors from Series B, showing confidence.

200
00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:19.160
<v Maya>That fast?

201
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.380
<v Alex>Yeah, but I doubt the simulations hit every target.

202
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.860
<v Maya>They say positive preclinical results, but no numbers.

203
00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:27.700
<v Alex>So the tech might be all hype?

204
00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.400
<v Maya>Not entirely; the big pharma letters show some traction.

205
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.460
<v Alex>Still, without real data I can't see the engine delivering.

206
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.720
<v Maya>If they do hit clinical gates, the market could shift.

207
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.640
<v Alex>I just think the proof will be in the trials, not the funding.

208
00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.660
<v Alex>The biggest move?

209
00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:48.020
<v Alex>A board‑level committee on national security.

210
00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:48.880
<v Maya>From TechCrunch.

211
00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:49.760
<v Alex>Seriously?

212
00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:53.780
<v Maya>The CFO, Colette Kress, now interim CEO leads it.

213
00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:57.020
<v Alex>The committee meets quarterly, chaired by Suzanne Johnson.

214
00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:01.040
<v Maya>It pulls the existing security and trade teams under its umbrella.

215
00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:03.420
<v Alex>So they’re just tightening oversight?

216
00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:05.260
